---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Side Projects · Javier Saldana"
  description="StatStack: an NFL analytics platform with a machine learning prediction pipeline, built end to end."
>
  <p class="label">Projects</p>
  <h1>Side projects</h1>
  <p>
    Side projects are where I explore what my professional work doesn't cover.
    They are deliberately secondary to the <a href="/work/">professional
    systems</a>, but each one shipped for real.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>StatStack</h2>
    <p class="meta">
      <a href="https://github.com/saldanaj27/StatStack">github.com/saldanaj27/StatStack</a>
      · live demo offline during the NFL offseason
    </p>
    <p>
      A data-first NFL decision engine: one page per matchup with every signal
      that matters (trends, ML predictions, player metrics, matchup context) and
      none of the articles, hot takes, or ads that bury it everywhere else. I
      built it to scratch a real recurring annoyance: weekly fantasy and betting
      decisions meant piecing together data from four or five ad-heavy sites.
    </p>
    <p>
      Under the hood it's a React SPA over a Django REST backend with PostgreSQL
      and Redis, ingesting 100K+ records across 1,600+ NFL games over six
      seasons. The prediction pipeline is a three-model scikit-learn ensemble
      (RandomForest for winners, GradientBoosting for spreads, Ridge for totals)
      trained on 44 engineered features per game, with database-backed model
      versioning so feature additions were measured rather than guessed at. It
      was my first serious Django project and my first ML project, which was
      the point: the goal was learning the full shape of a production system,
      from nflverse seed pipelines through query optimization (N+1 elimination,
      database-level aggregation, per-endpoint Redis caching) to CI-gated
      deploys with error tracking and uptime monitoring.
    </p>
    <p>
      Honest framing: a substantial learning-and-building project that reached
      production and real personal use during the 2025 NFL season, not a product
      with traction. Parts are deliberately over-engineered for a solo project;
      learning what production systems need was the goal.
    </p>
    <p class="tags">
      python · django · react · postgresql · redis · scikit-learn · docker
    </p>
  </section>

  <style>
    section {
